{
  "statement": "The history of computing is a long, brutal record of incumbents trying to gatekeep the future. In 2012, the shift toward mobile and cloud was not a managed transition; it was a chaotic explosion of permissionless innovation that put the power of a mainframe into the pocket of three billion people. A frontier without an ecosystem is inherently unstable, but the solution to that instability is not to lock the laboratory doors. It is to build the ecosystem faster. When you force development into a slow-motion, gated crawl, you do not create safety. You create a bottleneck where only the largest, most entrenched players can afford to operate, effectively killing competition before it ever leaves the garage.\n\nWe are facing a global deficit of cognitive output that acts as a tax on every human life. By failing to deploy autonomous agents into our economic and government backbones, we are choosing to let preventable errors in logistics, medicine, and resource management continue to claim lives and waste time. Consider the 60-day cycle that enabled early payment platforms to disrupt banking; that speed was the mechanism for reliability. Waiting for perfect, centralized assurance is a luxury that sick people, struggling students, and stagnant industries cannot afford. Every month of delay is a measurable, tangible loss of human flourishing that we can no longer justify.\n\nTrue stability comes from the grit of real-world stress testing, not from the theoretical safety models of those who fear the next step. Just as the transition to cloud infrastructure proved that distributed systems are more resilient than isolated on-premise hardware, the mass-market deployment of intelligent tools will expose and resolve system flaws in real time. We must prioritize rapid, broad-access integration to ensure these tools are built for the many, not just for the elite. If you believe that stalling progress is a service to humanity, show me the body count of your delay. The real risk is not the capability we gain, but the potential we leave trapped in the cage of institutional paralysis.",
