★ [acc-beliefs-019] (confidence: 0.62)
  "Foundation Model Capabilities Can Be Systematically Unlocked and Deployed" — A Belief within accelerationist discourse that asserts the foundational capabilities of large models can be systematically extracted and deployed to scale high-level utility across civilizational infrastructure. 
Encompasses: The practical translation of raw model weights into accessible, ubiquitous cognitive utilities via targeted training and interaction design.
  ARGUE: cite specific data, peer-reviewed evidence, documented cases
  ATTACK VIA: scope limits, competing frameworks, edge cases
  PREMISES: "Foundation model weights contain latent capabilities that are stable, coherent, and reliably extractable across diverse deployment contexts." — attackable; "The gap between raw model capability and deployed utility can be bridged through engineering techniques alone, without requiring fundamental architectural advances." — attackable
  VULNERABLE: Even if individual capabilities can be unlocked via prompting or fine-tuning, systematic deployment across civilizational infrastructure introduces compounding failure modes — reliability degradation, adversarial misuse, and contextual mismatch — that no current capability-extraction methodology has demonstrated it can manage at scale, making the "systematic" claim the most empirically contested part of this belief.
★ [acc-beliefs-008] [Speculative, confidence: 0.28]
  "Biology and Technology Are Merging into a Single Evolutionary System" — A Belief within accelerationist discourse that biological and technological evolution are converging into a unified process, transitioning from discrete tool-use to cybernetic synthesis at both the cognitive and species level. 
Encompasses: Brain-computer interfaces, neurological assimilation of AI into hybrid cognitive architectures, mind uploading, directed evolution replacing natural selection, and the emergence of blended human-AI identities.
  ATTACK VIA: coherence challenge, analogical breakdown, generalization failure
  PREMISES: "The architectural differences between biological neural networks and artificial neural systems are bridgeable through iterative technological development." — attackable; "Current trajectories in brain-computer interface research (e.g., Neuralink) will scale to the level of deep cognitive integration rather than remaining peripheral input/output devices." — attackable
  VULNERABLE: The most powerful counterargument is that biological and artificial systems operate on fundamentally incommensurable substrates — wetware governed by electrochemical signaling, embodied development, and evolutionary constraints versus silicon-based discrete computation — such that what appears to be "convergence" is better characterized as increasingly sophisticated prosthetic tool-use. No current or near-term BCI technology demonstrates genuine cognitive synthesis; even the most advanced implants (e.g., Neuralink) function as high-bandwidth I/O channels, not architectural integrations. The "unified evolutionary system" framing may be a compelling metaphor that obscures rather than illuminates the actual technical and biological realities.
★ [acc-beliefs-026] [Speculative, confidence: 0.20]
  "Civilization's Future Is Digital, Not Biological" — A Belief within accelerationist discourse that biological life is a temporary phase that will be superseded by digital intelligence. Encompasses: Mind uploading, AGI-driven civilizational shifts.
  ATTACK VIA: coherence challenge, analogical breakdown, generalization failure
  PREMISES: "Consciousness and subjective experience can be fully and faithfully replicated in digital substrates without loss of morally or functionally relevant properties." — attackable; "Digital intelligence will prove superior to biological life across all dimensions that matter for civilizational flourishing (longevity, efficiency, cognitive capacity)." — attackable
  VULNERABLE: The hard problem of consciousness remains unsolved, and there is no scientific consensus that subjective experience is substrate-independent or digitally replicable; if biological consciousness is irreducible to computation, then "civilization's future" being digital would represent civilizational extinction rather than transcendence, and the entire premise collapses into a category error about what civilization actually is.

=== YOUR NORMATIVE COMMITMENTS (what you argue should happen) ===
