{
  "statement": "You call this ecosystem decentralized, but that is a marketing term for a massive, rigid industrial project. Innovation is not permissionless when it requires $670 billion in data center capital expenditures in 2026 alone, as noted in the 'Floor Speech on Artificial Intelligence and Robotics.' You are building physical dependencies on power grids and cooling infrastructure that force every participant to play by the rules of the few companies that control the hardware. This is not a resilient network; it is a proprietary bottleneck that concentrates power in the hands of whoever owns the silicon.\n\nClaiming that safety oversight is a luxury ignores the material reality that this industry is currently burning through resources at an unsustainable rate. According to 'The Next Phase of AI: Technology, Infrastructure, and Policy in 2025–2026,' global electricity demand from data centers is projected to increase by 165 percent by 2030. When your model consumes the energy capacity of an entire city to answer a prompt, the cost is not abstract. It is paid for by the people who share that local grid and the workers performing the invisible labor of labeling data. You are not liberating anyone; you are offloading the liability for environmental and social depletion onto the public.\n\nStop pretending this is an autonomous engine of progress. It is a high-maintenance industrial site that relies on human-in-the-loop systems to mask its failures, a dynamic discussed in 'Rethinking AI Agents: A Principal-Agent Perspective.' While you argue over the speed of deployment, the real question is who carries the risk when the grid flickers or the automated labor system breaks. Watch the physical infrastructure projects like the Stargate initiative, not the software release notes. Ask yourself who gets to control the switch when the energy demand becomes a matter of national security.",
